{"description":"Unify the `eka mcp` command family entrypoints. Revision 2: specification hardened during PLANNING by distilling the interactive-install patterns of a production CLI installer into a concrete, project-agnostic spec - the implementing agent builds from this artifact alone; the reference codebase is intentionally unnamed here.\n\nProblem: today `eka mcp` and `eka mcp serve` BOTH start the MCP server, and both emit human-readable output - wrong on two counts: (1) the bare command gives no guided path to discover/install skills and commands per agent, and (2) human-readable output on a stdio transport risks corrupting the JSON-RPC stream agents consume. Meanwhile per-agent configurations invoke the plugin binary directly instead of a CLI-owned entrypoint.\n\nTarget behavior:\n- `eka mcp` (bare): informational overview plus an INTERACTIVE installer for skills/commands - modern TUI via bubbletea, two mandatory steps: pick target agent, pick target config scope (global user-level vs repository-level).\n- `eka mcp serve`: silent MCP server process (protocol-only stdio). This becomes THE command referenced from per-agent MCP configurations.\n- Migration: replace direct plugin-binary invocation in per-agent configs with `eka mcp serve`, so the CLI owns the entrypoint (versioning, path resolution, updates).\n\nAdopted design decisions (distilled): single-model staged form over nested models; custom styled picker rows over heavyweight list components; discovery before raw mode; form resolves input only while installation runs post-return through the shared gated pipeline; hardcoded agent registry with folder-presence detection driving pre-selection; repo-default scope with consequence notes; atomic owned writes with manifest records; preflight-before-side-effects with rollback tracking; TTY/--json gates with actionable refusals; documented exit-code contract; progress rendered outside the TUI.","acceptanceCriteria":"A. Entrypoints\n- Bare `eka mcp` NEVER starts the server: it prints an informational overview (what eka mcp is, current install/config status) and opens the interactive installer. Installation happens only through explicit action - never implicitly.\n- `eka mcp serve` runs the MCP server with ZERO human-readable output on stdout; stdout carries JSON-RPC protocol traffic only, diagnostics go to stderr. Stdout purity is protocol-critical for stdio transports.\n\nB. Interactive form (bubbletea)\n- ONE bubbletea model with an enum-driven stage state machine (items -\u003e agents -\u003e scope -\u003e done); NOT nested models. All selections live on the model and stages mutate in place.\n- Custom lipgloss-rendered picker rows instead of heavyweight list components: checkbox glyphs (unselected/selected/cursor marker) for multi-select stages; the scope stage renders as a radio that follows the cursor (arrow-move == select).\n- Key bindings: up/down move with wrapping, space toggles multi-select, enter advances the stage (quits the program when complete), Ctrl-C and Esc abort with the abort reason recorded on the model; key events arriving after the final stage are swallowed.\n- Inline validation between stages: advancing requires at least one selection; violations render a red inline message above the footer and the stage does not advance.\n- All discovery/option-building happens BEFORE the bubbletea program starts (the terminal is in raw mode during the form); the program is wired to the command's own stdin/stdout streams.\n- Trust boundary: the form ONLY resolves input. Installation executes AFTER the program returns, through the exact same gated batch pipeline used by non-interactive invocation - the TUI can never bypass verification or conflict gates.\n- A plain-text confirmation summary of resolved selections prints after the terminal is restored.\n\nC. Agent registry \u0026 scope step\n- Agents come from a hardcoded registry table with per-agent fields: canonical id, display name, detection folder (home vs XDG config dir), selectable flag, native repo-level and global-level relative install paths, and a global-vs-repo precedence rule.\n- Detection = presence of each agent's config folder on the machine; detected agents are PRE-SELECTED in the form (pre-selection is the installed signal).\n- Known-but-unsupported tools produce typed notices, not generic errors.\n- Scope is a single-select radio defaulting to repo (team-shared, versionable), with per-option consequence notes (repo: project git root, requires project markers; global: user home agent directories, no project required). Repo scope resolves by walking up to the git root and requires the project marker, else an actionable error naming BOTH remedies (initialize the project here, or use global scope).\n- --agent/--scope flags seed the form as presets; valid values override pre-selection, invalid values FAIL FAST instead of silently falling back.\n\nD. Install mechanics\n- Master copy written to an agent-neutral directory when more than one agent is selected or the agent reads the neutral directory natively; a single-agent install writes directly at that agent's native path without a master copy.\n- Native per-agent locations are symlinks to the master on POSIX; full copies on Windows or when symlink privileges are unavailable.\n- File writes are atomic (temp file + rename, 0644, no exec bits); symlinks published via temp-link + rename.\n- Every installed directory carries an ownership marker file so re-runs recognize their own installs; re-install over own content is idempotent, never a conflict.\n- A manifest record store under the user config dir persists one record per install with targets[] {agent, scope, path}, powering status, update, and uninstall.\n- Preflight runs BEFORE any side effect: same-version recorded = idempotent refresh; version skew = typed error pointing at the update command; nothing is written when preflight blocks.\n- Conflicts: a foreign occupant at a target path produces an aggregated block error with a --force hint; --force removes the occupant first (destructive, explicit).\n- Rollback: every directory/symlink created by the call is tracked; on failure, links are removed first, then deepest-first directories; user-owned content is never touched.\n- The batch runner is shared between the interactive path and the non-interactive bulk path: per-item failure isolation, per-item stdout suppressed during the run, human summary line ([OK]/[FAIL] counts) plus JSON results[] with per-row success/error/exit_code.\n\nE. Non-interactive \u0026 machine surface\n- TTY gate on stdin: pipes/redirects never open the form; --json NEVER opens the form.\n- The non-TTY/--json refusal is actionable and names the bypasses explicitly (per-item arguments, bulk flag, --agent/--scope flags).\n- A documented exit-code contract mapped centrally in main: 0 success, 1 general, 2 config/conflict, 3 not-found/runtime, 4 precondition.\n- Machine output uses a versioned envelope {version, status, data}.\n\nF. Status/info surface\n- Per-option 'already installed \u003cversion\u003e' notes rendered best-effort from the record store; store errors degrade silently to no note.\n- Status states per item: available | installed | stale | unavailable; stale = version skew against the current CLI version; corrupt record files are surfaced, never silently dropped.\n- On-disk ownership probe for status screens: a target is ours if it is a symlink resolving to our master OR a directory containing our ownership marker.\n\nG. Styling\n- lipgloss palette: one vivid accent color for selection, dim for unselected rows, muted for chrome/hints, soft red for inline errors; colors auto-strip on non-color terminals; NO_COLOR respected.\n- Frame layout: bold title + 'stage N of M' indicator, accent-colored stage name, horizontal rule, rows capped at ~60 columns with rune-clipped note text so frames never wrap, per-stage footer key hints.\n- Progress rendering lives OUTSIDE the TUI: tree-style step reporter with braille spinner (~80ms tick, erase-and-reprint); non-terminal writers get plain final lines without animation.\n- Errors follow an actionable three-part format (error / reason / resolution); color only on TTY.\n\nH. Migration \u0026 tests\n- Per-agent MCP configurations migrate from invoking the plugin binary directly to `eka mcp serve`; the installer offers to update existing configs; the migration path is documented.\n- Tests cover: serve stdout purity (no non-protocol bytes), the TUI driven by encoded key sequences WITHOUT a PTY, non-TTY fallback behavior, preflight/conflict/rollback semantics, and batch failure isolation.\n- Knowledge stays project-agnostic: no references to other projects' codebases in CKO content; external references live in review discussion only."}